    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-9001",
      "problem": "What is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234518/assets/99bb0ea67859fbd109442fcf698427ea43452087b4523b7202a760a2a8b27052.png)",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3 ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 36\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 36\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt[3]{27} = 3\\:\\textrm{in}.\n$$",
